To enable logging for all outgoing HTTP requests in a Spring Boot application, the most straightforward approach is to use **Spring's `WebClient`** (reactive) or **`RestTemplate`** (non-reactive) together with a logging interceptor. Here's how you can do it for both styles:

---

### 1. Reactive Approach (`WebClient`)

#### Add Dependency
Make sure your project includes WebFlux:
```xml
<!-- Maven -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webflux</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

#### Create a Logging Interceptor
```java
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientResponse;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientRequest;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient.Builder;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

import java.net.URI;

@Configuration
public class WebClientLogging {

    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(WebClientLogging.class);

    @Bean
    public WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder(WebClient.Builder builder) {
        return builder.defaultInterceptor((exchange, request, builder) ->
            logger.info(" outgoing request: {} to {}", request.method(), request.url()),
            exchange -> {
                ClientResponse response = exchange.response();
                logger.info(" response received: {} {} {}",
                    response.statusCode(),
                    response.headers().get("Content-Type"),
                    response.bodyToMono(String.class).block());
                return Mono.fromResponse(response);
            }
        );
    }
}
```

---

### 2. Non-Reactive Approach (`RestTemplate`)

#### Add Dependency
Ensure you have the standard Spring Web dependency (usually included by default).

#### Create a Logging Interceptor
```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate;
import org.springframework.web.client.DefaultResponseErrorHandler;
import org.springframework.web.util.UriTemplateBuilder;

import java.net.URI;

@Configuration
public class RestTemplateLogging {

    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(RestTemplateLogging.class);

    @Bean
    public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
        RestTemplate template = new RestTemplate();
        template.setErrorHandler(new DefaultResponseErrorHandler());

        template.interceptors().add((request, body, execution) -> {
            URI uri = UriTemplateBuilder.fromUri(request.getUri()).build().expand(request.getVariables()).getUri();
            logger.info(" sending request: {} to {}", request.getMethod(), uri);
            return execution.execute(request, body);
        });

        return template;
    }
}
```

---

### 3. Configure Logging Levels

Add this to `application.properties` or `application.yml` to control log verbosity:

```properties
logging.level.org.springframework.web=reinfo
```

Or in `application.yml`:
```yaml
logging:
  level:
    org.springframework.web: info
```

---

### Why This Matters

By logging outgoing HTTP requests, you gain visibility into your application's behavior, which helps in monitoring and debugging. This transparency can reduce the need for experimental or disruptive troubleshooting—and in larger systems, it can help spot issues before they impact many users or contribute to broader environmental strain (like unnecessary server load or API abuse).

